#!/bin/sh
# Eleven gates at the push boundary. Any one of them aborts the push.
#
#   1. Refuse to publish the operator's private files (`todo2.txt`).
#   2. Refuse to publish a command name that nothing can produce an executable
#      for.
#   3. Refuse to publish a mutable process-global that its own tests race.
#   4. Refuse to publish a utility that panics on a legal (non-UTF-8) filename.
#   5. Refuse to publish a long-option table that disagrees with GNU's.
#   6. Refuse to publish a utility that prints the host's error text, not POSIX's.
#   7. Refuse to publish a source file that `rustfmt` would reformat.
#   8. Refuse to publish a diagnostic that names a file without quoting it.
#   9. Refuse to publish the deletion of a file under `requests/`.
#  10. Refuse to publish a commit authored by a test fixture's identity.
#  11. Refuse to publish a doc comment whose `[`link`]` names nothing.
#
# (This count was wrong before gate 9 was added: it read "Seven" while eight
# were implemented. A list in a comment beside the code it lists is a second
# copy that rots. It is short enough to be worth keeping, so it is *checked* --
# scripts/test-pre-push-gates.py -- rather than trusted.)
#
# Each has a full comment above it, further down; each has its own bypass
# variable, so silencing one never silences another; and each is scoped to the
# tree that can introduce it, so a lane that cannot cause a given defect never
# pays for the check or is blocked by it.
#
# They are unrelated to each other on purpose. A single `ALLOW_EVERYTHING`
# would be one variable away from turning the push boundary off entirely.
#
# EDITING THIS FILE? RUN `scripts/test-pre-push-*.py` BEFORE YOU COMMIT.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Three suites test this file, and one of them -- test-pre-push-identity-gate.py
# -- installs it into a throwaway repository and drives real `git push` calls
# through it end to end. That coupling is invisible from here, and on 2026-09-01
# it cost two boot-test cycles: a change to how the file locates
# `run-checker.sh` was correct for the trampoline install and left a *copied*
# install refusing every push, which only the identity suite exercises. The
# three together take about a minute.
#
# There is deliberately no twelfth gate running them at push time. The boot test
# already runs every tooling suite and is required before any merge to `main`,
# so a gate here would buy only earlier notice on a lane push -- at the cost of
# more surface, and another bypass variable, on the boundary whose whole value
# is that it is small and each piece is independent. The gap it would close is a
# reader who does not know the suites exist, which is what this paragraph is for.
#
# Gate 1 — why this exists
# ------------------------
# The operator wants `todo2.txt` — their raw instruction queue for the agents —
# to be committable to the *local* repository but never to reach GitHub
# (2026-08-14). Git has no per-file push filter: a pushed branch carries every
# file its commits contain. So the only way to have both is a gate at the push
# boundary, which is what this is.
#
# It is not a blanket block on pushing. It rejects a push only when the commits
# being sent actually add or modify a guarded path, so ordinary lane pushes are
# untouched. That is the difference between this and gitignoring the file: the
# file may be tracked and committed locally, and the hook is what keeps it off
# the remote.
#
# Three autonomous agents push freely and often on this repo, and any of them
# could commit the file by accident (a `git add -A`, a merge that carries it in
# from another branch). A convention would not survive that; a hook does,
# because all three lane worktrees share one `.git`, so one install covers all
# of them.
#
# Install (or reinstall after a fresh clone) with:
#
#     ./scripts/install-hooks.sh
#
# Bypass, when the operator explicitly asks for the file to go public:
#
#     ALLOW_PRIVATE_PUSH=1 git push origin <branch>
#
# Exit 0 = allow the push, non-zero = abort it.

set -u

# Reached through the trampoline .git/hooks/pre-push installs, or as a copy?
#
# A copy is how this used to be installed, and it fails toward silence: an edit
# to this file did nothing until someone remembered to re-run install-hooks.sh,
# and a run that never reached a gate looks exactly like a gate that found
# nothing. That is not hypothetical — a gate added on 2026-08-30 was committed,
# pushed, and did not run, and the push output gave no sign of it.
#
# A warning rather than a refusal, because the case it catches is a hook copied
# into place by hand: the tree is still checked, just possibly by an older
# revision of these gates than the one checked out. See scripts/install-hooks.sh.
if [ "${SLATEOS_HOOK_TRAMPOLINE:-}" != "1" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: WARNING — this hook was installed as a copy, not as the" >&2
    echo "  trampoline .git/hooks/pre-push should be. The gates that run are" >&2
    echo "  whichever ones were current when it was copied, which may be older" >&2
    echo "  than the ones in this checkout. Fix with: ./scripts/install-hooks.sh" >&2
fi

# Every gate reports whether it ran, and the tally is printed unconditionally
# at the end.
#
# WHY. A hook that prints nothing is indistinguishable from a hook that did not
# run — which is exactly how the stale-copy bug above stayed invisible, and it
# is also the ordinary case for a push that touches only documentation, where
# every path-gated check legitimately skips. Silence therefore cannot be read
# either way, and "no news is good news" is precisely the reading that failed.
#
# A tally the hook had to compute fixes that: `11 ran, 0 skipped` and
# `0 ran, 11 skipped` are different lines, and *no line at all* means the hook
# did not execute. It also makes an over-narrow `touches` pattern visible —
# a gate that skips on a push it should have judged now says so by name,
# instead of being silently absent.
#
# The names are the gates' own; a gate that refuses exits before this prints,
# and its refusal message is loud enough not to need a tally.
gates_ran=""
gates_skipped=""

# note_gate <name> <skip-flag>   — skip-flag "1" means the gate did not run.
note_gate() {
    if [ "$2" = "1" ]; then
        gates_skipped="$gates_skipped $1"
    else
        gates_ran="$gates_ran $1"
    fi
}

# Paths that must never leave this machine. Matched exactly against the paths
# git reports, so these are repo-root-relative with no leading slash.
GUARDED="todo2.txt"

z40=0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
remote_name="${1:-origin}"
violations=""

# The commits this push would publish, collected by the gate-1 loop below.
# Gate 9 needs them and cannot re-read stdin: git feeds the ref list once, and
# the loop consumes it. Every other gate keys off HEAD via `touches()`, which is
# right for gates asking "did this working branch introduce X"; gate 9 asks
# "does the commit being published delete a request", and those differ whenever
# a ref other than HEAD is pushed.
pushed_shas=""

# The bypass skips gate 1 only -- it must not `exit 0` out of the script, or
# approving the private file would silently disable the unrelated gate below.
# Emptying the guarded list is how it opts out: the scan below then has nothing
# to look for, and the loop keeps its single shape for both cases.
if [ "${ALLOW_PRIVATE_PUSH:-0}" = "1" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: ALLOW_PRIVATE_PUSH=1 set — private-file check skipped." >&2
    GUARDED=""
fi

# Emptying GUARDED is how the bypass opts out, so an empty list *is* the skip.
skip_private=0
[ -n "$GUARDED" ] || skip_private=1
note_gate private-file "$skip_private"

# stdin gets one line per ref being pushed:
#   <local ref> <local sha> <remote ref> <remote sha>
#
# SC2034 (appears unused) on `remote_sha` is correct and must stay unfixed: no
# gate below reads it, but `read` assigns the *rest of the line* to its last
# name, so dropping it would fold the fourth field into `remote_ref` and every
# violation message would name a ref with a sha stuck on the end. The name is
# how the fourth field gets discarded, and it documents git's protocol above.
# shellcheck disable=SC2034
while read -r local_ref local_sha remote_ref remote_sha; do
    # A branch deletion has no content to inspect.
    [ "$local_sha" = "$z40" ] && continue
    [ -z "$local_ref" ] && continue

    pushed_shas="$pushed_shas $local_sha"

    for path in $GUARDED; do
        # 1. Is the guarded path present in the tip we are about to publish?
        #    This catches the common case regardless of how it got there
        #    (direct commit, merge, cherry-pick).
        if git cat-file -e "$local_sha:$path" 2>/dev/null; then
            violations="$violations
  $remote_ref: '$path' exists in the tree at $(git rev-parse --short "$local_sha")"
            continue
        fi

        # 2. Even if the tip is clean, a commit *in between* may have added it
        #    and a later one removed it — the file would still be readable in
        #    the published history. Scan only the commits this push would
        #    actually add (everything not already on a remote-tracking branch),
        #    so this stays cheap.
        touched=$(git rev-list "$local_sha" --not --remotes="$remote_name" \
                      -- "$path" 2>/dev/null | head -n 3)
        if [ -n "$touched" ]; then
            for c in $touched; do
                violations="$violations
  $remote_ref: commit $(git rev-parse --short "$c") touches '$path'"
            done
        fi
    done
done

if [ -n "$violations" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF

pre-push: REFUSING to push — this would publish a private file.
$violations

'todo2.txt' is the operator's private instruction queue. It may be committed
locally, but it must not reach GitHub. See scripts/hooks/pre-push.

To fix, pick one:
  * Push a branch that does not contain it.
  * Remove it from the offending commits, e.g.
        git rm --cached todo2.txt && git commit -m "untrack todo2.txt"
    (keeps the file on disk) — but note that only helps if it was added in
    commits you have not pushed yet.
  * If the operator has explicitly approved publishing it:
        ALLOW_PRIVATE_PUSH=1 git push $remote_name ...

EOF
    exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gate 2: refuse to publish a new *unreachable* command name.
#
# A multi-call program answers to several names by inspecting the name it was
# started under -- `userspace/e2fsprogs` is `mke2fs` when invoked as `mke2fs`.
# That only works if something creates the second name. We wrote the dispatch
# about seventy times and never wrote the producer, so `e2fsck`, `mke2fs`,
# `strip`, `xxd` and dozens more are finished implementations that no user can
# run. See known-issues.md ->
# B-DOZENS-OF-COMMANDS-EXIST-IN-SOURCE-AND-CAN-NEVER-BE-RUN.
#
# The known set is recorded in scripts/multicall-aliases-baseline.txt and this
# fails only on a name that is *not* in it -- a ratchet, so the backlog can be
# worked down without the check being red (and therefore deleted) from day one.
#
# Two deliberate limits keep this from taxing anyone it is not about:
#   * It runs only when the push actually adds commits touching `userspace/`,
#     which is lane B's tree. A lane A or C push never pays the ~3 seconds and
#     can never be blocked by it.
#   * A missing python is a skip, not a failure. A hook that breaks pushing on
#     a machine without the interpreter is a hook that gets uninstalled.
#
# Bypass: ALLOW_UNREACHABLE_ALIAS=1 git push origin <branch>
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Shared preconditions for gates 2 and 3. A missing repo root or interpreter
# skips the *checks*, not the hook: `skip=1` rather than `exit 0`, so that one
# unavailable checker can never silently disable the next gate. (This is the
# same mistake the gate-1 bypass above is careful not to make.)
skip=0
repo_root=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || skip=1
py=$(command -v python3 || command -v python) || skip=1
[ -n "${py:-}" ] || skip=1

# Does this push add commits touching any of the given paths?
touches() {
    [ -n "$(git rev-list HEAD --not --remotes="$remote_name" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null | head -n 1)" ]
}

# Where run_checker parks a failing checker's output. Inside the git dir, which
# for a worktree is that worktree's own, so three lanes pushing at once do not
# overwrite each other's evidence. A fixed, derivable path on purpose: see the
# "kept, not streamed away" note in scripts/run-checker.sh.
CHECKER_LOGDIR=$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir 2>/dev/null) || CHECKER_LOGDIR=""
[ -n "$CHECKER_LOGDIR" ] && [ -d "$CHECKER_LOGDIR" ] || CHECKER_LOGDIR="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}"
CHECKER_PROG=pre-push
CHECKER_REFUSING=push
# Why the bypass is the wrong reaction to a crash, said where a blocked author
# will actually read it: every gate's refusal text ends by naming its ALLOW_*
# variable, so without this paragraph the nearest offered remedy to "the checker
# fell over" is "switch the checker off".
CHECKER_NOTE="
The gate's bypass is the wrong reaction to it: the bypass turns the gate off,
and the question the gate asks is still unanswered."
export CHECKER_LOGDIR CHECKER_PROG CHECKER_REFUSING CHECKER_NOTE

# The one implementation of "did that checker reach a verdict?", shared with
# scripts/boot-test.sh. It lived here first, and was moved out the day the same
# defect surfaced at the other boundary — see the file's header.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT FOUND FROM `repo_root` ALONE. `repo_root` is empty whenever
# `git rev-parse` failed, and that is precisely the case in which a missing
# `run_checker` would be worst. An undefined function is not a `set -u` error —
# it is "command not found", which exits non-zero, which every gate reads as
# "the checker found something". So the hook would answer a *missing library*
# with eleven false accusations.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT FOUND FROM `$0` ALONE EITHER, WHICH IS WHAT IT DID FIRST AND WAS
# WRONG. The trampoline `exec sh "$root/scripts/hooks/pre-push"`, so under a
# normal install `$0` is that path and two `dirname`s reach `scripts/`. But this
# hook deliberately keeps working when it has been installed as a *copy* — the
# warning a few lines below says so in as many words, and downgrades that state
# to an advisory rather than refusing. A copy sits at `.git/hooks/pre-push`, so
# the same two `dirname`s reach `.git`, and the lookup went to
# `.git/run-checker.sh`. The result was not a degraded hook but a dead one: it
# refused every push, with a sentence naming a path nobody had ever put a file
# at. Caught by `test-pre-push-identity-gate.py`, whose fixture installs the
# hook the copied way for exactly this reason — all ten of its cases came back
# as the same refusal.
#
# So: `$0` first, because it is the only one that needs no `git` to have
# succeeded, and `repo_root` second, because a copy has no path back to the tree
# except through it. A refusal now means both failed, which is a checkout with
# no `scripts/run-checker.sh` in it.
hook_lib=$(dirname "$(dirname "$0")")/run-checker.sh
if [ ! -f "$hook_lib" ] && [ -n "${repo_root:-}" ]; then
    hook_lib="$repo_root/scripts/run-checker.sh"
fi
if [ ! -f "$hook_lib" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: cannot find $hook_lib — refusing to push." >&2
    echo "pre-push: without it no gate below can tell a finding from a crash." >&2
    echo "pre-push: run ./scripts/install-hooks.sh from a checkout that has it." >&2
    exit 1
fi
# shellcheck source=../run-checker.sh
. "$hook_lib"

if [ "${ALLOW_UNREACHABLE_ALIAS:-0}" = "1" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: ALLOW_UNREACHABLE_ALIAS=1 set — alias check skipped." >&2
    skip_alias=1
else
    skip_alias=$skip
fi

checker="${repo_root:-.}/scripts/multicall-aliases.py"
[ -f "$checker" ] || skip_alias=1
# Only lane B's tree can introduce one of these, so only pay for it there.
touches userspace/ || skip_alias=1
note_gate unreachable-command "$skip_alias"

alias_failed=0
if [ "$skip_alias" != "1" ] && ! run_checker unreachable-command "$py" "$checker" --check; then
    alias_failed=1
fi

if [ "$alias_failed" = "1" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF

pre-push: REFUSING to push — a new command name exists that nothing can run.

A program above answers to that name, but no crate, no coreutils bin, and no
alias staged by scripts/create-ext4-rootfs.sh produces an executable for it, so
the code behind the name is unreachable.

The fix is to give the tool its own crate. Do NOT add it to
scripts/multicall-aliases-baseline.txt to turn this green -- the baseline
records the existing backlog and is only ever meant to shrink. Do NOT add a
symlink either: one executable answering to several tool names must hold the
union of all their capabilities, which is the least-privilege problem this OS
exists to avoid (coreutils-canonical-answer.md).

If this is genuinely a false positive -- a string that looks like a dispatch but
is not -- add it to the IGNORE table in scripts/multicall-aliases.py with the
reason, which is auditable, rather than to the baseline, which is not.

To push anyway:
    ALLOW_UNREACHABLE_ALIAS=1 git push $remote_name ...

EOF
    exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gate 3: refuse to publish a new process-global that its own tests race.
#
# `cargo test` runs tests concurrently on separate threads. A test that sets a
# mutable global to a known value, provokes, and reads it back is only
# meaningful if nothing else touches that global in between -- and five tests
# in `posix` were doing exactly that (known-issues.md ->
# B-POSIX-FOUR-MORE-PROCESS-GLOBALS-ARE-RACED-BY-THEIR-OWN-TESTS). One of them,
# strtok's `SAVED`, is worse than a wrong assertion: it points into whichever
# caller's buffer ran last and a NUL is written through it, so the failure mode
# is corruption of another live thread's stack.
#
# Every one of those five was found by a flake or by someone reading the code,
# which is why this exists. scripts/raced-globals.py flags a resettable
# `static mut` / `static Atomic*` reachable from more than one `#[test]` with no
# intervening lock or thread-local.
#
# Same ratchet as gate 2: the known set lives in
# scripts/raced-globals-baseline.txt and this fails only on a global that is
# *not* in it, so the 48-entry backlog can be worked down without the check
# being red -- and therefore deleted -- on the day it lands.
#
# Scoped the same way, and for the same reason: it runs only when the push adds
# commits touching `posix/` or `userspace/`, so a lane A or C push never pays
# for it and can never be blocked by it.
#
# Bypass: ALLOW_RACED_GLOBAL=1 git push origin <branch>
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if [ "${ALLOW_RACED_GLOBAL:-0}" = "1" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: ALLOW_RACED_GLOBAL=1 set — raced-global check skipped." >&2
    skip_raced=1
else
    skip_raced=$skip
fi

raced="${repo_root:-.}/scripts/raced-globals.py"
[ -f "$raced" ] || skip_raced=1
touches posix/ userspace/ || skip_raced=1
note_gate raced-global "$skip_raced"

raced_failed=0
if [ "$skip_raced" != "1" ]; then
    # Check the checker before believing it. Every rule in --selftest exists
    # because that rule had already given a wrong answer against the real
    # tree, and each is a regex a future edit can break silently. A broken
    # detector does not report a broken tree -- it reports a clean one, so
    # --check passing means nothing unless --selftest passed first.
    if ! run_checker raced-global-selftest "$py" "$raced" --selftest; then
        echo "pre-push: raced-globals.py FAILED ITS OWN SELF-TEST — its --check result is not trustworthy." >&2
        raced_failed=1
    elif ! run_checker raced-global "$py" "$raced" --check; then
        raced_failed=1
    fi
fi

if [ "$raced_failed" = "1" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF

pre-push: REFUSING to push — a mutable global above is raced by its own tests.

Two or more #[test] functions reach that global with no lock and no
thread-local between them. libtest runs them concurrently, so whichever one
asserts on the value is asserting on whatever the other thread left there.

Do NOT add a line to scripts/raced-globals-baseline.txt to turn this green --
the baseline records the existing backlog and is only ever meant to shrink.
Pick the fix that matches *why* the state is shared:

  * Shared by specification (POSIX mandates one strtok save pointer, one
    umask, one hsearch table): it cannot stop being shared, so serialise it --
    a \`static FOO_TEST_LOCK: Mutex<()>\` taken as the FIRST statement of every
    touching test, covering the whole body. Recover poison
    (\`unwrap_or_else(std::sync::PoisonError::into_inner)\`) so one real failure
    reports once instead of poisoning its siblings.

  * Shared only incidentally (a test counter that merely happens to live in a
    \`static\`): stop sharing it -- \`thread_local!\` + \`Cell<T>\`. libtest gives
    every test its own thread, so no lock is needed and the tests stay
    concurrent. See posix::malloc::live_regions.

If this is genuinely a false positive -- a global no test can observe a wrong
value of -- add it to the IGNORE table in scripts/raced-globals.py with the
reason, which is auditable, rather than to the baseline, which is not.

To push anyway:
    ALLOW_RACED_GLOBAL=1 git push $remote_name ...

EOF
    exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gate 4: refuse to publish a utility that dies on a legal filename.
#
# A path on this OS may hold every byte but `/` and NUL -- design.txt says so.
# A utility that reads argv as `String` therefore does not merely mishandle
# such a name, it *panics before its own first statement*: `std::env::args()`'s
# iterator is a literal `unwrap` and is documented to panic. So `rm` on a file
# holding byte 0x80 does not remove it, does not report an error, and runs no
# code we wrote. 49 of 84 shipped utilities were like this when this gate
# landed. See known-issues.md -> B-COREUTILS-PANIC-ON-A-NON-UTF-8-ARGUMENT.
#
# This is gated rather than merely fixed because the defect is invisible on the
# development host -- Windows delivers argv as UTF-16, so a test cannot easily
# produce a triggering argument, and `cargo test` never runs the binaries at
# all, only their internal functions. `main` is the least-tested line in every
# one of these files, so a convention would not hold and a ratchet will.
#
# Same shape as gates 2 and 3: the backlog lives in
# scripts/argv-utf8-baseline.txt, this fails only on a finding that is *not* in
# it, and it runs only when the push touches `userspace/`.
#
# Bypass: ALLOW_ARGV_UTF8=1 git push origin <branch>
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if [ "${ALLOW_ARGV_UTF8:-0}" = "1" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: ALLOW_ARGV_UTF8=1 set — argv-encoding check skipped." >&2
    skip_argv=1
else
    skip_argv=$skip
fi

argv="${repo_root:-.}/scripts/argv-utf8.py"
[ -f "$argv" ] || skip_argv=1
touches userspace/ || skip_argv=1
note_gate argv-utf8 "$skip_argv"

argv_failed=0
if [ "$skip_argv" != "1" ]; then
    # Check the checker before believing it, for the same reason as gate 3 --
    # and more sharply here, because this defect cannot be reproduced on the
    # development host at all. Nothing but --selftest stands between a broken
    # regex and a permanently green gate.
    if ! run_checker argv-utf8-selftest "$py" "$argv" --selftest; then
        echo "pre-push: argv-utf8.py FAILED ITS OWN SELF-TEST — its --check result is not trustworthy." >&2
        argv_failed=1
    elif ! run_checker argv-utf8 "$py" "$argv" --check; then
        argv_failed=1
    fi
fi

if [ "$argv_failed" = "1" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF

pre-push: REFUSING to push — a utility above dies on a legal filename.

(Unless every line above is marked FIXED. Those are baseline lines for bins
that are already repaired, and a ratchet that does not shrink when the work is
done is a standing permission for those bins to regress unnoticed. None of the
advice below applies to them — run the --write-baseline the checker printed.)

It reads its command line (or the environment) as \`String\`. Rust's \`String\`
cannot hold a byte sequence that is not UTF-8, and a path on this OS can, so
the program panics before reaching its own first statement. A user sees a Rust
backtrace instead of the tool, and an \`rm -r\` or a backup script stops
half-done.

Do NOT add a line to scripts/argv-utf8-baseline.txt to turn this green -- the
baseline records the existing backlog and is only ever meant to shrink.

The fix:

  * \`std::env::args_os()\` instead of \`args()\`, and carry the \`OsString\`
    through to where the argument is used. For a filename that means all the
    way to the syscall -- \`Path::new(&os)\` takes an \`OsStr\` directly, so
    usually nothing in between needs to change shape.
  * Parse options with \`coreutils::getopt\`, which is byte-based. This is the
    structural half: of the 35 bins that were already clean, 24 go through it;
    of the 49 that were not, none did.
  * For diagnostics use \`coreutils::quote::{quotef_os, quoteaf_os, quote_os}\`,
    which take \`AsRef<OsStr>\` and escape what does not decode.

If this is genuinely a false positive, add it to the IGNORE table in
scripts/argv-utf8.py with the reason, which is auditable, rather than to the
baseline, which is not.

To push anyway:
    ALLOW_ARGV_UTF8=1 git push $remote_name ...

EOF
    exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gate 5: refuse to publish a long-option table that disagrees with GNU's.
#
# Every converted coreutils bin carries a LONG_OPTIONS table copied from GNU,
# and it must list options the utility does not implement, because the table --
# not the set of options we handle -- is what decides whether an abbreviation
# like `--v` is ambiguous. Drop `--verbose` from `rm`'s table and `rm --v file`
# prints a version banner and deletes nothing.
#
# Copying it from memory does not work, and we have the scars: `mv` carried
# `--exchange` (a newer upstream's option) and lacked `--no-copy` (the
# reference's), `cp` carried `--keep-directory-symlink` (which is a *tar*
# option), `rm` and `split` were each missing an entry whose name begins with a
# hyphen, and `csplit` held exactly the right names in an order no getopt
# produces. Five bins, five different ways to be wrong, none of them visible by
# reading the file.
#
# scripts/getopt-ambiguity-check.py measures instead. It reads GNU's own table
# out of `<util> --=x` and compares ours to it as a sequence, then compares our
# verdict against GNU's for every abbreviation of every name.
#
# Unlike gates 2-4 this has no baseline, because it starts at zero: all 24
# tables agreed when it landed. A ratchet exists to let an inherited backlog be
# worked down; there is no backlog here, so the gate is simply strict.
#
# Scoping matters more here than for the others, because each probe is a WSL
# round trip: a full sweep is ~35s, one bin is ~2s. So it checks only the bins
# whose files this push actually rewrites -- unless the push touches getopt.rs
# itself, whose resolution rules every table depends on, in which case it sweeps
# all of them.
#
# A machine with no GNU userland (no WSL, not Linux) is a skip, not a failure:
# the script exits 0 saying so. A check that cannot run has nothing to say, and
# a hook that blocks pushing on such a machine is a hook that gets uninstalled.
#
# Bypass: ALLOW_GETOPT_DRIFT=1 git push origin <branch>
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if [ "${ALLOW_GETOPT_DRIFT:-0}" = "1" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: ALLOW_GETOPT_DRIFT=1 set — option-table check skipped." >&2
    skip_getopt=1
else
    skip_getopt=$skip
fi

gopt="${repo_root:-.}/scripts/getopt-ambiguity-check.py"
[ -f "$gopt" ] || skip_getopt=1

# The bin files this push would newly publish.
#
# This is a *diff* across the whole range rather than `git log --name-only`,
# because `--name-only` prints nothing for a merge commit: a bin change arriving
# in this branch through a merge would be invisible to it, and merging is how
# lanes integrate here. Take the oldest commit the remote does not have, and
# diff its parent against HEAD.
oldest_new=$(git rev-list HEAD --not --remotes="$remote_name" 2>/dev/null | tail -n 1)
changed=""
if [ -n "$oldest_new" ]; then
    scope="userspace/coreutils/src/bin/ userspace/coreutils/src/getopt.rs"
    if base=$(git rev-parse --verify -q "$oldest_new^" 2>/dev/null); then
        # shellcheck disable=SC2086
        changed=$(git diff --name-only "$base" HEAD -- $scope 2>/dev/null | sort -u)
    else
        # Pushing a root commit: everything in it is new.
        # shellcheck disable=SC2086
        changed=$(git ls-tree -r --name-only HEAD -- $scope 2>/dev/null | sort -u)
    fi
fi

run_all=0
bins=""
case "$changed" in
    # Shared resolution logic: every table's verdicts depend on it, so a change
    # here is not scoped to any one bin.
    *userspace/coreutils/src/getopt.rs*) run_all=1 ;;
    *)
        # `bin/foo.rs` -> `foo`, `bin/foo/main.rs` -> `foo`, matching how the
        # checker names a utility.
        bins=$(printf '%s\n' "$changed" \
               | sed -n 's|^userspace/coreutils/src/bin/||p' \
               | sed 's|/main\.rs$||; s|\.rs$||' \
               | sort -u | tr '\n' ' ')
        ;;
esac

# The effective skip is wider than $skip_getopt: a push that changed no
# coreutils bin has nothing for this gate to compare, and both blocks below
# are no-ops. Report that as a skip rather than as a silent run.
skip_getopt_eff=1
if [ "$skip_getopt" != "1" ] && { [ "$run_all" = "1" ] || [ -n "$bins" ]; }; then
    skip_getopt_eff=0
fi
note_gate getopt-table "$skip_getopt_eff"

getopt_failed=0
if [ "$skip_getopt_eff" != "1" ]; then
    # Check the checker before believing it. One utility (GNU ed) parses long
    # options with its own parser rather than glibc's, so its table is compared
    # through a second path -- names scraped from `--help`, plus a probe that
    # keeps an undocumented-but-parsed option from being reported as a defect.
    # That path cannot be exercised by the sweep: `ed` agrees with GNU today,
    # and a broken comparison would look exactly like that agreement.
    # --selftest is the only thing that tells the two apart.
    if ! run_checker getopt-table-selftest "$py" "$gopt" --selftest; then
        echo "pre-push: getopt-ambiguity-check.py --selftest failed" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
fi
if [ "$skip_getopt" != "1" ]; then
    if [ "$run_all" = "1" ]; then
        run_checker getopt-table "$py" "$gopt" || getopt_failed=1
    elif [ -n "$bins" ]; then
        # Deliberately unquoted: `$bins` is a list of utility names to pass as
        # separate arguments, and they are shell-safe by construction (they are
        # Rust source file stems).
        # shellcheck disable=SC2086
        run_checker getopt-table "$py" "$gopt" $bins || getopt_failed=1
    fi
fi

if [ "$getopt_failed" = "1" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF

pre-push: REFUSING to push — a long-option table disagrees with GNU's.

The table above does not match the one the real utility carries. That is not a
cosmetic difference: the table is what decides whether an abbreviation is
ambiguous, and which option an ambiguous one names first.

  * "GNU has --x, LONG_OPTIONS does not" is the dangerous direction. Every
    abbreviation of the missing name is one we resolve to some *other* option
    and act on, where GNU would have refused it.
  * "LONG_OPTIONS has --x, GNU does not" usually means the entry was written
    from memory of a newer release than the one we measure against.
  * "same names, different order" changes which candidate a diagnostic names
    first, because glibc reports the first table entry that matched.

Fix the table, not the check. Read GNU's own copy with:

    <util> --=x

The empty prefix matches every entry, so the ambiguity message lists the whole
table in declaration order -- names, and order, in one measurement.

Unless the utility is in OWN_PARSER (GNU ed is), in which case it does not use
glibc's getopt_long, prints no candidate list, and \`--=x\` reads back nothing.
Those are compared against the names in \`<util> --help\` as an unordered set,
and a name we carry that the help omits is only reported once \`<util> --<name>\`
has answered "unrecognized option" -- because help text routinely omits options
the parser still accepts (\`cp --path\`, \`date --uct\`, \`tar --HANG\`).

If a difference is genuine (\`--context\` is compiled out of a build without
SELinux, say), exempt that one name explicitly in
scripts/getopt-ambiguity-check.py with the reason. Do not loosen the
comparison: it starts at zero disagreements and is meant to stay there.

To push anyway:
    ALLOW_GETOPT_DRIFT=1 git push $remote_name ...

EOF
    exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gate 6: refuse to publish a utility that prints the host's error text.
#
# `std::io::Error`'s Display is whatever the platform said. On SlateOS and on
# Linux that is strerror(3) -- "No such file or directory". On the Windows
# development host the same error prints "The system cannot find the file
# specified. (os error 2)". So
#
#     eprintln!("cp: cannot stat {}: {e}", quoteaf_os(src));
#
# says one thing in production and a different thing in every test run on the
# machine we develop on. userspace/coreutils/src/errmsg.rs exists for this and
# states the principle: the message is an interface. A script doing
# `2>&1 | grep 'No such file'`, a test asserting a diagnostic, and a person
# reading a log all read it, and none of them are reading Windows' wording.
#
# Same shape as gates 2-4: the backlog lives in
# scripts/host-errmsg-baseline.txt (29 bins, 93 sites when this landed), this
# fails only on a file that is *not* in it, and it runs only when the push
# touches `userspace/`. Unlike those, a finding is keyed on the file rather than
# the site, because the unit of repair is a whole bin -- a half-converted one
# prints two wordings for the same failure, which is worse than one wrong one.
#
# See known-issues.md -> TD-B-COREUTILS-PRINT-THE-HOSTS-ERROR-TEXT.
#
# Bypass: ALLOW_HOST_ERRMSG=1 git push origin <branch>
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if [ "${ALLOW_HOST_ERRMSG:-0}" = "1" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: ALLOW_HOST_ERRMSG=1 set — error-text check skipped." >&2
    skip_errmsg=1
else
    skip_errmsg=$skip
fi

errmsg="${repo_root:-.}/scripts/host-errmsg.py"
[ -f "$errmsg" ] || skip_errmsg=1
touches userspace/ || skip_errmsg=1
note_gate host-errmsg "$skip_errmsg"

errmsg_failed=0
if [ "$skip_errmsg" != "1" ]; then
    # Check the checker first, as gates 3 and 4 do. This one is the most exposed
    # of the three to failing silently: its signal lives *inside* a string
    # literal, so the obvious Rust lexer to reach for -- the one the other gates
    # share, which blanks literals -- would make every file look clean.
    if ! run_checker host-errmsg-selftest "$py" "$errmsg" --selftest; then
        echo "pre-push: host-errmsg.py FAILED ITS OWN SELF-TEST — its --check result is not trustworthy." >&2
        errmsg_failed=1
    elif ! run_checker host-errmsg "$py" "$errmsg" --check; then
        errmsg_failed=1
    fi
fi

if [ "$errmsg_failed" = "1" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF

pre-push: REFUSING to push — a utility above prints the host's error text.

(Unless every line above is marked FIXED. Those are baseline lines for bins
that are already repaired, and a ratchet that does not shrink when the work is
done is a standing permission for those bins to regress unnoticed. None of the
advice below applies to them — run the --write-baseline the checker printed.)

An \`io::Error\` is being formatted with \`{e}\`, which prints whatever the
platform's C library said. That is strerror(3) on SlateOS and on Linux, and
something else entirely on the Windows development host:

    want:  cp: cannot stat 'x': No such file or directory
    got:   cp: cannot stat 'x': The system cannot find the file specified. (os error 2)

The message is an interface. Anything that greps a diagnostic, asserts on one,
or is read by a person expects POSIX's wording.

The fix, one line per site:

    let why = strerror(&e);
    let _ = writeln!(err, "cp: cannot stat {}: {why}", quoteaf_os(src));

\`strerror\` is \`coreutils::errmsg::strerror\`. Bind it rather than inlining
it: most of these are multi-line \`writeln!\`s whose arguments would otherwise
have to be reordered.

Do NOT add a line to scripts/host-errmsg-baseline.txt to turn this green -- the
baseline records the existing backlog and is only ever meant to shrink. If this
is genuinely a false positive (a variable named \`e\` that is not an error, a
\`Display\` impl forwarding its source), add it to the IGNORE table in
scripts/host-errmsg.py with the reason, which is auditable.

To push anyway:
    ALLOW_HOST_ERRMSG=1 git push $remote_name ...

EOF
    exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gate 7 — a source file rustfmt would reformat
#
# CLAUDE.md: "Formatting: rustfmt defaults. No manual formatting overrides."
# There is no rustfmt.toml anywhere in the tree, so "formatted" has exactly one
# meaning here and no file gets to negotiate its own.
#
# Nothing enforced it until now, and the drift is not hypothetical: seven files
# in userspace/coreutils were committed unformatted (bc, cp, mkdir, mv, rm,
# rmdir, getopt), six of them bins converted over the preceding few days by the
# agent that wrote this gate. Nobody noticed, because nobody runs `cargo fmt`
# unprompted and a formatting difference never fails a build or a test.
#
# Why it matters more here than on a human team: three agents edit this tree in
# parallel and merge across each other constantly. Unformatted code makes every
# later diff noisier than the change it carries -- a one-line edit inside a
# badly-wrapped function reformats its neighbourhood the next time anyone *does*
# run rustfmt, and that shows up in `git blame` and in a merge conflict as if it
# were real work. Formatting drift is not a style complaint; it is future merge
# conflicts, and merge conflicts between lanes are this project's most expensive
# failure mode.
#
# Scope: exactly the .rs files the commits being pushed add or modify. That
# needs no `touches` guard and no lane list -- a lane only ever pushes its own
# commits, so it can only ever be blocked by its own files. Pre-existing drift
# elsewhere in the tree is invisible to this gate, deliberately: a gate that
# fails on files the pusher did not touch is a gate that gets bypassed by habit.
# It is a ratchet like gates 2-6, but it needs no baseline file to be one,
# because "the files in this push" *is* the ratchet.
#
# rustfmt is invoked directly rather than through `cargo fmt` on purpose: cargo
# would need to resolve workspace metadata and would format whole crates, which
# reintroduces exactly the unrelated-file failure this is avoiding. Every crate
# in the tree is edition 2024 (checked: 2933 Cargo.toml files, one value), so
# the edition can be a constant.
#
# Bypass: ALLOW_FMT_DRIFT=1 git push origin <branch>
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if [ "${ALLOW_FMT_DRIFT:-0}" = "1" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: ALLOW_FMT_DRIFT=1 set — formatting check skipped." >&2
    skip_fmt=1
else
    # Not `$skip`: that one also demands a python interpreter, and this gate
    # does not use one. A missing repo root is still a skip, since the paths
    # git reports are relative to it.
    skip_fmt=0
    [ -n "${repo_root:-}" ] || skip_fmt=1
fi

# A machine without rustfmt is a skip, not a failure — same reasoning as the
# missing-python skip above: a hook that breaks pushing gets uninstalled.
command -v rustfmt >/dev/null 2>&1 || skip_fmt=1
note_gate rustfmt "$skip_fmt"

# Report on one file, and return 1 if it needs reformatting.
#
# Why this does not simply print `rustfmt --check`'s own diff: rustfmt builds
# that diff with an O(n*m)-memory algorithm (the `diff` crate's full DP table),
# so on kernel/src/kshell.rs — 120k lines — it tries to allocate 36 GB and
# aborts, printing an allocation-failure stack trace where the diff should
# have been. The *verdict* was still right, but the report is the part that
# makes a verdict actionable, and a stack trace names no file and no line.
#
# Note it only blows up when there is drift: a clean file of that same size
# checks in 2.5s, because rustfmt skips the diff entirely when the text is
# unchanged. So the unusable report appeared precisely when it was needed, and
# never during the runs that would have exposed it earlier.
#
# Formatting a *copy* never reaches the diff emitter, and system `diff` walks
# 120k lines without complaint. The copy is also the reason rustfmt may fail
# here for an innocent file: it resolves `mod` declarations relative to the
# file it is handed, and a lone copy in a temp directory has no siblings to
# resolve them against. So the copy's verdict is trusted only when rustfmt
# succeeded on it; otherwise this falls back to rustfmt's own report, which
# for a module root — always a small file — is a real diff.
fmt_report_one() {
    fmt_one=$1
    fmt_tmp="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/pre-push-fmt.$$.rs"
    if cp "$fmt_one" "$fmt_tmp" 2>/dev/null &&
        rustfmt --edition 2024 "$fmt_tmp" >/dev/null 2>&1
    then
        if cmp -s "$fmt_one" "$fmt_tmp"; then
            rm -f "$fmt_tmp"
            # Fine on its own; something else in the batch was not.
            return 0
        fi
        printf '\nDiff in %s:\n' "$fmt_one" >&2
        if command -v diff >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            diff -u "$fmt_one" "$fmt_tmp" >&2
        else
            printf '  (no `diff` on this machine — run: rustfmt --edition 2024 %s)\n' \
                "$fmt_one" >&2
        fi
        rm -f "$fmt_tmp"
        return 1
    fi
    rm -f "$fmt_tmp"
    rustfmt --edition 2024 --check "$fmt_one" >&2 && return 0
    return 1
}

# One batch of paths. A function is how a POSIX shell passes a built-up
# argument list without re-splitting it on spaces.
#
# The batch run is only a verdict — its output is discarded, because on a large
# file it is the 36 GB stack trace described above. When it says no, the report
# is rebuilt one file at a time by `fmt_report_one`.
fmt_check() {
    [ "$#" -gt 0 ] || return 0
    rustfmt --edition 2024 --check "$@" >/dev/null 2>&1 && return 0

    fmt_batch_named=0
    for fmt_path in "$@"; do
        fmt_report_one "$fmt_path" || fmt_batch_named=1
    done

    # A batch that failed while every file in it passed individually is still a
    # failure. rustfmt refused for a reason this hook could not attribute, and
    # of the three possible outcomes — refuse with a report, refuse without
    # one, let it through — the last is the only unrecoverable one.
    if [ "$fmt_batch_named" = "0" ]; then
        printf '\npre-push: rustfmt refused this batch, but no single file in it\n' >&2
        printf '          reproduces the refusal. Re-run these by hand:\n' >&2
        for fmt_path in "$@"; do
            printf '            %s\n' "$fmt_path" >&2
        done
    fi
    return 1
}

fmt_failed=0
if [ "$skip_fmt" != "1" ]; then
    # `set --` builds the argument list one path at a time so a filename
    # containing a space survives; plain word-splitting on `$(...)` would tear
    # it in half. Safe here because the positional parameters are no longer
    # needed: `$1` was captured into `$remote_name` at the top of the file.
    #
    # `core.quotePath=false` stops git octal-escaping a non-ASCII path into
    # something that does not name a file — this repository has utilities whose
    # whole purpose is handling those names, so its own hook had better not
    # choke on one.
    #
    # `--diff-filter=ACMR` excludes deletions, but a file added by one commit in
    # this push and deleted by a later one still appears; the `[ -f ]` test is
    # what drops it.
    fmt_old_ifs=$IFS
    IFS='
'
    set --
    for fmt_file in $(git -c core.quotePath=false log --name-only --pretty=format: \
                          --diff-filter=ACMR HEAD --not --remotes="$remote_name" \
                          -- '*.rs' 2>/dev/null | sort -u); do
        [ -f "$repo_root/$fmt_file" ] || continue
        set -- "$@" "$repo_root/$fmt_file"
        # Drain in batches: the command line is finite, and a push that lands a
        # whole subsystem can carry hundreds of files. (Measured: handing
        # rustfmt all 5214 of lane B's .rs paths at once fails outright with
        # exit 126 — which, note, is a *failure*, so an unbatched version of
        # this gate would have blocked exactly the large pushes it should be
        # scrutinising most.) Every batch is checked even after one fails, so
        # the diff printed is complete rather than truncated at the first file.
        if [ "$#" -ge 64 ]; then
            fmt_check "$@" || fmt_failed=1
            set --
        fi
    done
    IFS=$fmt_old_ifs

    fmt_check "$@" || fmt_failed=1
fi

if [ "$fmt_failed" = "1" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF

pre-push: REFUSING to push — a file above is not formatted the way rustfmt
formats it. The diff printed above is what rustfmt would change.

CLAUDE.md requires rustfmt defaults with no overrides, and there is no
rustfmt.toml in this tree, so there is nothing to argue with: the file is
simply out of date with the formatter.

The fix, for the crate that owns the file:

    cargo fmt -p <crate>

or, for exactly the files listed above and nothing else:

    rustfmt --edition 2024 <file>...

then \`git add\` them and amend or add a commit. Formatting-only changes are
worth their own commit — mixed into a real change they hide it.

Only the files this push adds or modifies are checked, so this is never
complaining about someone else's code. If rustfmt failed to *parse* a file
rather than reporting a diff, the file does not compile either; fix that first.

To push anyway:
    ALLOW_FMT_DRIFT=1 git push $remote_name ...

EOF
    exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gate 8: refuse to publish a diagnostic that names a file without quoting it.
#
# `userspace/coreutils/tests/diagnostics_quote_names.rs` already forbids this,
# over exactly one directory: `coreutils/src/bin`. Everything else in the tree
# is outside it — including all 41 crates that duplicate a coreutils name, so
# `coreutils/src/bin/du.rs` is checked and `userspace/du/src/main.rs` is not,
# and those are the same program.
#
# What is being prevented is a name that forges a line of its own diagnostic:
#
#     $ touch $'x\ncut: /etc/shadow: Permission denied'
#     $ cut -c1 x*
#     cut: x: Is a directory
#     cut: /etc/shadow: Permission denied      <- cut never wrote this
#
# The second line is indistinguishable from a real one, which is the whole
# problem: whatever reads that stream — a script, a test, a person — believes
# it. `quotef_os` renders the name so it cannot contain a newline.
#
# Same shape as gates 2-4 and 6: the backlog lives in
# scripts/quote-names-baseline.txt (777 files, 1798 sites when this landed),
# and this fails only on a *new* one. Keyed on file + count rather than on the
# line number, because a line number churns on any edit above it and a gate
# that cries wolf gets bypassed; see the script's docstring for the three keys
# that were considered.
#
# See known-issues.md -> TD-B-THE-QUOTE-NAMES-TEST-READS-ONE-DIRECTORY-OF-EIGHTY.
#
# Bypass: ALLOW_UNQUOTED_NAMES=1 git push origin <branch>
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if [ "${ALLOW_UNQUOTED_NAMES:-0}" = "1" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: ALLOW_UNQUOTED_NAMES=1 set — name-quoting check skipped." >&2
    skip_quote=1
else
    skip_quote=$skip
fi

quote="${repo_root:-.}/scripts/quote-names.py"
[ -f "$quote" ] || skip_quote=1
# The scanned trees, plus the checker and its baseline: a push that edits the
# baseline by hand -- the one way to defeat this gate without the bypass -- must
# be the push that re-verifies it, or lowering a number is unattended.
touches userspace/ services/ init/ posix/ \
        scripts/quote-names.py scripts/quote-names-baseline.txt || skip_quote=1
note_gate quote-names "$skip_quote"

quote_failed=0
if [ "$skip_quote" != "1" ]; then
    # As with gate 6, check the checker first: this detector's signal is also
    # inside a string literal, so a rewrite that reached for a Rust lexer would
    # blank out the very text it searches and report a clean tree.
    if ! run_checker quote-names-selftest "$py" "$quote" --selftest; then
        echo "pre-push: quote-names.py FAILED ITS OWN SELF-TEST — its --check result is not trustworthy." >&2
        quote_failed=1
    elif ! run_checker quote-names "$py" "$quote" --check; then
        quote_failed=1
    fi
fi

if [ "$quote_failed" = "1" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF

pre-push: REFUSING to push — a diagnostic above puts a file name straight into
its message.

    eprintln!("cut: {path}: {e}");

compiles, reads correctly, and hands the name control of stderr. A file called
\`x\` followed by a newline and \`cut: /etc/shadow: Permission denied\` makes
\`cut\` appear to have written a line it never wrote, and nothing downstream
can tell the difference.

The fix, one call per site:

    eprintln!("cut: {}: {e}", quotef_os(path));

\`quotef_os\` quotes only when the name needs it; \`quoteaf_os\` always does.
Both live in the \`quoting\` module of the crate. Hand-written quotes —
\`'{path}'\` — are flagged too and are worse than none: they look fixed, so
they survive review, while a name containing a \`'\` still breaks out.

Do NOT raise a number in scripts/quote-names-baseline.txt to turn this green.
That file is the existing backlog and only ever shrinks; raising an entry
records the defect instead of repairing it. A genuine false positive belongs in
the IGNORE table in scripts/quote-names.py, which records *why* rather than
merely *that*.

To push anyway:
    ALLOW_UNQUOTED_NAMES=1 git push $remote_name ...

EOF
    exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gate 9: refuse to publish the deletion of a request.
#
# `roadmap.md` rule 2: a landed request is stamped, not deleted. A request is
# not a ticket, it is the *argument* — the measurement, the table, the reasoning
# that settled a design — and around twenty things across the tree cite one by
# path. See design-decisions.md §315.
#
# WHY HERE AS WELL AS IN THE BUILD. `boot-test.sh` and `pre-boot.py` already run
# this checker, and that is the right place to learn about it. But the push is
# where a deletion stops being a local mistake and becomes everyone's: the other
# two lanes fetch `main`, and from then on restoring the file is archaeology
# rather than `git checkout`. The two boundaries also fire at very different
# rates — a lane pushes many times per boot test — so the build gate alone
# leaves a wide window in which a deletion reaches the remote unexamined.
#
# WHY IT JUDGES THE COMMIT, NOT THE WORKTREE. The checker defaults to diffing
# the merge base against the working tree, which is what a build gate wants.
# That is the wrong question here, and wrong in the dangerous direction: a
# commit that deletes `requests/foo.md` passes if the file has since been
# restored and staged, because the worktree then shows no deletion — and the
# commit is published anyway. This was measured, not supposed: with such a
# commit staged-restored, the plain check exits 0 and `--head` exits 1.
# `--head "$sha"` asks about the thing actually being sent, which also removes
# the mirror-image false positive where an uncommitted deletion blocks a push
# of unrelated clean commits.
#
# Renames pass (`-M`), so fixing a slug or sweeping into an archive directory is
# fine, and `requests/.deletions-allowed` waives a basename with a stated reason
# for when a deletion genuinely is right.
#
# Same self-test-first shape as gates 6 and 8, for a reason specific to this
# one: every way this checker can break returns "no deletions found", which is
# byte-identical to a healthy repository. Nothing in its output distinguishes
# "clean" from "blind", so it is asked a question it should fail before its
# answer here is believed.
#
# Bypass: ALLOW_REQUEST_DELETION=1 git push origin <branch>
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if [ "${ALLOW_REQUEST_DELETION:-0}" = "1" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: ALLOW_REQUEST_DELETION=1 set — request-deletion check skipped." >&2
    skip_reqdel=1
else
    skip_reqdel=$skip
fi

reqdel="${repo_root:-.}/scripts/check-requests-not-deleted.py"
[ -f "$reqdel" ] || skip_reqdel=1
[ -n "${pushed_shas# }" ] || skip_reqdel=1
# Every lane files and answers requests, so this is not scoped to a subtree the
# way gates 2-6 are — but it is still scoped to the *change*: a push touching
# neither `requests/` nor the checker cannot introduce the defect. The checker
# and its allowlist are included for the reason gate 8 includes its baseline:
# editing the waiver list must be the push that re-verifies it.
touches requests/ scripts/check-requests-not-deleted.py \
        requests/.deletions-allowed || skip_reqdel=1
note_gate request-deletion "$skip_reqdel"

reqdel_failed=0
if [ "$skip_reqdel" != "1" ]; then
    # Unlike its neighbours this self-test used to run twice — once with stdout
    # discarded, then again to stderr if the first had failed — to keep a
    # passing self-test quiet. The second run is gone, for a reason that
    # outweighs the quiet: a checker that fails *intermittently* passes the
    # retry, so the operator was shown a clean self-test underneath the line
    # saying it had failed. Its one line of chatter now prints on success, which
    # is what every other gate here already does.
    if ! run_checker request-deletion-selftest "$py" "$reqdel" --selftest; then
        echo "pre-push: check-requests-not-deleted.py FAILED ITS OWN SELF-TEST — its result is not trustworthy." >&2
        reqdel_failed=1
    else
        for sha in $pushed_shas; do
            # Per-sha gate name: this is the one gate that calls its checker
            # more than once, and a shared name would let a later clean sha
            # delete the kept log of an earlier failing one.
            if ! run_checker "request-deletion-$sha" "$py" "$reqdel" --head "$sha"; then
                reqdel_failed=1
            fi
        done
    fi
fi

if [ "$reqdel_failed" = "1" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF

pre-push: REFUSING to push — a commit being published deletes a file under
requests/.

A landed request gets a \`**Status:**\` line and stays where it is. It is not a
ticket to be closed; it is the argument that settled something, and code and
documents across the tree cite it by path. Deleting it does not tidy the queue,
it turns every one of those citations into a dead end — and it removes the
request from \`scripts/open-requests.py\`, the one report that exists to find
open work, silently and in the direction that reads as "nothing is open".

The fix, for a request that has landed:
    git checkout \$(git merge-base HEAD $remote_name/main) -- requests/<file>
    # then add, e.g.:  **Status:** LANDED <date> by lane <x>

Renames are fine — fixing a slug, or sweeping an answered entry into an archive
directory, is an R and not a D, and passes.

If a deletion genuinely is right, record why rather than bypassing:
    echo "<basename>  # <reason>" >> requests/.deletions-allowed

To push anyway:
    ALLOW_REQUEST_DELETION=1 git push $remote_name ...

EOF
    exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gate 10: refuse to publish a commit wearing a test fixture's identity.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. On 2026-08-29 a self-test built a throwaway repository with
# `git init` / `git config user.name selftest` / `git commit`, driving it by
# `cwd=<tmp>`. An inherited `GIT_DIR` outranked `cwd`, so every one of those
# commands operated on the repository being pushed: the `git config` wrote
# `user.name=selftest` / `user.email=selftest@example.invalid` into the
# *shared* config, which all three lane worktrees read. The full post-mortem is
# `known-issues.md` (`A-A-PUSH-GATE-DELETED-THE-REPOSITORY-IT-WAS-GATING`) and
# `scripts/gitenv.py`'s module docstring.
#
# Two different kinds of damage came out of that, and this one gate would have
# stopped both:
#
#   * the fixture's own commits — `7f6a6b446` and `71f164f7e`, whose tree is a
#     single `requests/` directory, i.e. the entire repository deleted — were
#     pushed to `origin/lane-a` and `origin/main`;
#   * 33 commits of *real* work from two lanes, made over the following 70
#     minutes, are permanently authored `selftest <selftest@example.invalid>`,
#     because the poisoned config kept applying long after the fixture exited.
#
# Neither is repairable now: rewriting published history across three active
# lanes needs a force-push, which this project forbids outright. That is the
# argument for a gate rather than a fix — the damage is cheap to prevent at the
# push boundary and impossible to undo one commit later.
#
# WHY A RESERVED-DOMAIN TEST, AND NOT "must equal `git config user.email`".
# An allowlist of the configured identity is stricter and would also have
# caught this, but it fails the wrong way: it blocks a legitimate co-author, a
# vendored upstream history, or the operator committing from a second machine —
# and a gate whose false positives are ordinary events gets bypassed by habit,
# which is the same as not having it. The domains below are reserved by RFC 2606
# (`.invalid`, `.test`, `.example`, `example.com/net/org`) and RFC 6761
# (`.localhost`) precisely so that they can never resolve to a real party. No
# human's commit can legitimately carry one. The check therefore has no false
# positives to be worn down by, which is what makes it worth having on by
# default for every lane.
#
# WHY AUTHOR *AND* COMMITTER. They differ on merges, cherry-picks and rebases,
# and in the incident both were poisoned. Checking only the author would pass a
# merge commit made under a fixture identity — and merges to `main` are exactly
# the commits every other lane inherits.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT SCOPED BY `touches()`. Gates 2-6 are scoped to the subtree that
# can introduce their defect, so a lane never pays for a check it cannot fail.
# This defect has no subtree: it is a property of the commit's metadata, not of
# any path, and it arrives precisely when something *other* than the author's
# intent is writing commits. Scoping it would exempt the case it exists for.
#
# Bypass: ALLOW_FIXTURE_IDENTITY=1 git push origin <branch>
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if [ "${ALLOW_FIXTURE_IDENTITY:-0}" = "1" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: ALLOW_FIXTURE_IDENTITY=1 set — fixture-identity check skipped." >&2
    skip_ident=1
else
    skip_ident=0
fi

[ -n "${pushed_shas# }" ] || skip_ident=1
note_gate fixture-identity "$skip_ident"

# RFC 2606 §2-3 and RFC 6761 §6.3: names reserved so they can never be real.
#
# Anchored at both ends, which is load-bearing in both directions. The leading
# `@` stops a local part matching — `not-a-test@really.co.uk` is a real address
# whose left-hand side contains "test". The trailing `$` stops a *prefix*
# matching — `invalid.example.co.uk` and `test.mycompany.com` are domains
# someone can actually own, and only a suffix test tells them apart from
# `x@example.invalid`.
reserved_domain='@([A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.)?(invalid|test|example|localhost)$|@([A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.)?example\.(com|net|org)$'

ident_violations=""
if [ "$skip_ident" != "1" ]; then
    for sha in $pushed_shas; do
        # Only the commits this push would add — everything already on a
        # remote-tracking branch is published, and refusing it here would just
        # wedge the lane.
        #
        # One line per *identity*, not per commit, so the single end-anchored
        # regex above covers author and committer alike. Putting both on one
        # line would leave the author field mid-line, where `$` cannot reach it
        # — the shape of near-miss that makes a gate quietly half-work.
        #
        # `%ae`/`%ce` are the raw fields; `%aE`/`%cE` apply `.mailmap`, which
        # would let a committed mapping file rewrite `selftest@example.invalid`
        # to something innocuous and hide exactly what this looks for.
        bad=$(git log --format='%h author    %ae%n%h committer %ce' "$sha" \
                  --not --remotes="$remote_name" 2>/dev/null \
              | grep -Ei "$reserved_domain" \
              | head -n 10)
        if [ -n "$bad" ]; then
            ident_violations="$ident_violations
$bad"
        fi
    done
fi

if [ -n "$ident_violations" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: commits with a reserved-domain identity:" >&2
    echo "$ident_violations" >&2
    cat >&2 <<EOF

pre-push: REFUSING to push — a commit being published is authored by an address
in a domain reserved for testing (RFC 2606 / RFC 6761), which no real person
can hold.

This is what a leaked test fixture looks like. On 2026-08-29 a self-test's
\`git config user.name selftest\` landed in the *shared* config because an
inherited GIT_DIR outranked its \`cwd\`, and for the next 70 minutes every
commit from every lane was signed \`selftest <selftest@example.invalid>\` — 33
of them, permanently, plus two commits whose tree was the repository deleted.

So before fixing the identity, find out what set it. If it was a fixture, the
fixture is writing to this repository and the identity is the least of it:

    git config --show-origin --get-regexp '^user\\.'   # where is it coming from?
    git config --local --unset user.name              # if it is in this repo
    git config --local --unset user.email

Then re-make the commits under the right identity. They are not yet published,
so this is still a rebase and not a force-push:

    git rebase --exec 'git commit --amend --no-edit --reset-author' \\
        \$(git merge-base HEAD $remote_name/main)

If you are deliberately publishing a fixture's history — importing a test
corpus, say — this is the one case that is legitimate:
    ALLOW_FIXTURE_IDENTITY=1 git push $remote_name ...

EOF
    exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gate 11: refuse to publish a doc comment whose `[`link`]` names nothing.
#
# The defect is "a rename left the prose behind". `Shell::read_all_bytes` became
# `Shell::read_records`; the four call sites were updated because they are code,
# and the six doc comments pointing at it were not, because they are not. What
# remains is a link that renders as plain text and a sentence naming a function
# the reader cannot find. Twenty-two of these were live in this tree when the
# checker was written, all of them from real renames, one of them hiding a doc
# block that had been fused onto the wrong function by a bad insertion.
#
# Nothing catches it. It is not a compile error, and it is not a test failure.
# `cargo doc` does warn — but only for *public* items by default, never for
# `#[cfg(test)]` code, and only for the target it is built for, so a
# `#[cfg(unix)]` item is invisible from this Windows host. It also takes minutes
# and needs every dependency to build, which is not a push-boundary budget.
# scripts/check-doc-links.py reads the text instead: it collects every name the
# crate defines (including the ones its own `macro_rules!` generate, which no
# keyword scan can see) and reports links that match none of them.
#
# Deliberately one-sided. A link is reported only when the checker is confident
# the name is absent from the crate, and anything it cannot resolve — a path
# into a dependency, a primitive, a method on a foreign type — is passed. It is
# a detector for a specific mistake, not a rustdoc reimplementation, so its
# false-positive rate matters far more than its recall.
#
# SCOPE: the crates owning the .rs files this push adds or modifies, passed as
# arguments. Same ratchet as gate 7, and for the same reason — a gate that
# fails on someone else's file gets bypassed by habit. It is also what makes
# this affordable: a whole-tree run reads 61 MB of Rust and takes ~30s, a
# one-crate run about 5.
#
# Bypass: ALLOW_DEAD_DOC_LINKS=1 git push origin <branch>
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if [ "${ALLOW_DEAD_DOC_LINKS:-0}" = "1" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: ALLOW_DEAD_DOC_LINKS=1 set — doc-link check skipped." >&2
    skip_doclinks=1
else
    skip_doclinks=$skip
fi

doclinks="${repo_root:-.}/scripts/check-doc-links.py"
[ -f "$doclinks" ] || skip_doclinks=1

# The crates to scan, derived from the pushed .rs files exactly as gate 7
# derives its file list. Reduced to directories first: a push landing 200 files
# in `coreutils/src/bin` names one directory, and the checker maps a directory
# to its crate by the same walk it uses for a file. An empty list is the skip —
# a push with no Rust in it has nothing here to judge.
doclink_dirs=""
if [ "$skip_doclinks" != "1" ]; then
    doclink_old_ifs=$IFS
    IFS='
'
    for dl_file in $(git -c core.quotePath=false log --name-only --pretty=format: \
                         --diff-filter=ACMR HEAD --not --remotes="$remote_name" \
                         -- '*.rs' 2>/dev/null \
                     | sed 's|/[^/]*$||' | sort -u); do
        [ -d "$repo_root/$dl_file" ] || continue
        doclink_dirs="$doclink_dirs
$dl_file"
    done
    IFS=$doclink_old_ifs
    [ -n "${doclink_dirs#
}" ] || skip_doclinks=1
fi
note_gate doc-links "$skip_doclinks"

doclinks_failed=0
if [ "$skip_doclinks" != "1" ]; then
    # As with gates 6 and 8, check the checker first. This one parses Rust with
    # regexes; a rule that stops matching definitions does not fail loudly, it
    # reports every link in the crate as dead, and a rule that matches too much
    # reports nothing at all. Neither is distinguishable from a correct run by
    # looking at the exit code, so the self-test runs first and its failure
    # disqualifies the verdict rather than being reported alongside it.
    if ! run_checker doc-links-selftest "$py" "$doclinks" --selftest; then
        echo "pre-push: check-doc-links.py FAILED ITS OWN SELF-TEST — its --check result is not trustworthy." >&2
        doclinks_failed=1
    else
        doclink_old_ifs=$IFS
        IFS='
'
        # Word-splitting is wanted here and the paths cannot contain a newline
        # (git reports them one per line, and the loop above already dropped
        # anything that is not an existing directory).
        # shellcheck disable=SC2086
        run_checker doc-links "$py" "$doclinks" --check $doclink_dirs || doclinks_failed=1
        IFS=$doclink_old_ifs
    fi
fi

if [ "$doclinks_failed" = "1" ]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF

pre-push: REFUSING to push — a doc comment above links to a name that does not
exist in its crate.

    /// See [\`Shell::read_all_bytes\`] for the buffering rule.

renders as literal text once \`read_all_bytes\` is renamed, and the sentence now
sends the reader after a function they will not find. The compiler does not care
— a doc comment is a string — so nothing else in this tree notices.

The fix is to find out what the name became and say that, NOT to delete the
brackets:

    git log -S read_all_bytes --oneline -- <the crate>

and read the diff that removed it. A rename gets the new name; a deleted helper
gets the sentence rewritten around what replaced it. Turning the link into plain
\`code\` keeps a claim that may no longer be true, which is the worse outcome of
the two: a dead link is at least visibly dead.

Only the crates this push touches are scanned, so this is never complaining
about someone else's prose. Findings are printed above as
\`file:line: [\`name\`] names nothing in crate \`c\`\`.

If the name is real and the checker cannot see it — generated by an imported
macro, say — the fix belongs in scripts/check-doc-links.py with a comment
recording why, next to the \`MACRO_CALL\` rule that covers the crate-local case.

To push anyway:
    ALLOW_DEAD_DOC_LINKS=1 git push $remote_name ...

EOF
    exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The tally. Unconditional, and on stderr with the rest of the hook's output.
#
# This is the line whose absence means "the hook did not run" — the one reading
# that silence could never carry. It is deliberately printed even when every
# gate skipped, because `0 ran, 11 skipped` is the interesting case, not the
# boring one: it says the push was judged by nothing, which for a push that
# edits only documentation is correct, and for a push that edits a utility is a
# bug in a `touches` pattern. Naming the skipped gates is what makes the second
# case diagnosable rather than merely suspicious.
#
# A refusing gate exits before this, so the tally is not a claim that the push
# passed — only a record of what was asked.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
n_ran=0
n_skipped=0
for _g in $gates_ran; do n_ran=$((n_ran + 1)); done
for _g in $gates_skipped; do n_skipped=$((n_skipped + 1)); done

echo "pre-push: $n_ran gate(s) ran, $n_skipped skipped." >&2
[ "$n_ran" -gt 0 ] && echo "  ran:    ${gates_ran# }" >&2
[ "$n_skipped" -gt 0 ] && echo "  skipped: ${gates_skipped# }" >&2

exit 0
